This is a less common option. Each stored queue contains the newest value and the previous queue as its second item. Pushing creates a new two-item queue, while popping simply moves back to the saved previous queue.

The queue nesting gives constant-time operations, but it is less familiar than rotating values. An empty queue is kept as an empty slice, so the first push and the final pop work normally.

```go
type QueueStack struct {
    queue []interface{}
}

func NewQueueStack() *QueueStack {
    return &QueueStack{
        queue: make([]interface{}, 0),
    }
}

func (qs *QueueStack) push(x int) {
    previous := qs.queue
    qs.queue = []interface{}{x, previous}
}

func (qs *QueueStack) pop() int {
    value := qs.queue[0].(int)
    previous := qs.queue[1].([]interface{})
    qs.queue = previous
    return value
}

func (qs *QueueStack) top() int {
    return qs.queue[0].(int)
}

func (qs *QueueStack) isEmpty() bool {
    return len(qs.queue) == 0
}
```

All four operations take O(1) time. The nested queues hold O(n) values and links, so the space cost is O(n). This code uses `interface{}` because each small queue stores both an integer and another queue.

This version keeps the simulated stack order in `mainQueue`. During `push`, the new value goes into the empty helper queue first, followed by all old values. Swapping the queues leaves the newest value at the front.

The input is not changed because the structure owns both queues. For example, pushing `4` after `[3, 2, 1]` creates `[4, 3, 2, 1]`.

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <utility>

class QueueStack {
private:
    std::queue<int> mainQueue;
    std::queue<int> helperQueue;

public:
    QueueStack() {
    }

    void push(int x) {
        helperQueue.push(x);

        while (!mainQueue.empty()) {
            helperQueue.push(mainQueue.front());
            mainQueue.pop();
        }

        std::swap(mainQueue, helperQueue);
    }

    int pop() {
        int value = mainQueue.front();
        mainQueue.pop();
        return value;
    }

    int top() {
        return mainQueue.front();
    }

    bool isEmpty() {
        return mainQueue.empty();
    }
};
```

Each `push` moves the existing values, so it costs O(n). `pop`, `top`, and `isEmpty` are O(1). The extra space is O(n). The first push needs no movement, which also handles an initially empty stack.

Here the queue stays in normal insertion order. To remove the stack top, move all but the last value into the second queue. The remaining value is the newest one. `top` does the same movement but puts that value back afterward.

Edge cases are covered for one value and for repeated `top` calls, because `top` restores the queue before returning.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

class QueueStack {
    private Queue<Integer> current;
    private Queue<Integer> spare;

    public QueueStack() {
        current = new ArrayDeque<>();
        spare = new ArrayDeque<>();
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        current.add(x);
    }

    public int pop() {
        while (current.size() > 1) {
            spare.add(current.remove());
        }

        int answer = current.remove();
        Queue<Integer> temporary = current;
        current = spare;
        spare = temporary;
        return answer;
    }

    public int top() {
        while (current.size() > 1) {
            spare.add(current.remove());
        }

        int answer = current.peek();
        spare.add(current.remove());

        Queue<Integer> temporary = current;
        current = spare;
        spare = temporary;
        return answer;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return current.isEmpty();
    }
}
```

`push` and `isEmpty` are O(1). `pop` and `top` take O(n) because they move older values. The queues together use O(n) space.

A single `deque` is enough here. After adding a value, rotate all older values behind it. This keeps the newest value at the front, so the front acts like the stack top.

Dry run: after `push(1)`, `push(2)`, and `push(3)`, the queue is `[3, 2, 1]`. A pop returns `3` immediately.

```python
from collections import deque

class QueueStack:
    def __init__(self):
        self.queue = deque()

    def push(self, x):
        self.queue.append(x)
        old_size = len(self.queue) - 1

        for _ in range(old_size):
            self.queue.append(self.queue.popleft())

    def pop(self):
        return self.queue.popleft()

    def top(self):
        return self.queue[0]

    def isEmpty(self):
        return not self.queue
```

`push` takes O(n), while `pop`, `top`, and `isEmpty` take O(1). The queue stores O(n) values. Calls to `pop` and `top` are assumed to happen only when the structure is not empty.

